{"page":699,"blocks":[{"x":0.75517,"y":0.05257,"w":0.19635,"h":0.01535,"fs":0.01535,"n":2,"k":1,"t":"11.6 Conic Sections 685"},{"x":0.36409,"y":0.09347,"w":0.59169,"h":0.02882,"fs":0.01279,"n":2,"k":1,"t":"If the parabola opens downward, with its focus at (0, -p) and its directrix the line y = p, then Equations (1) become"},{"x":0.5445,"y":0.13158,"w":0.0598,"h":0.02139,"fs":0.01279,"n":1,"k":1,"t":"y = - x2"},{"x":0.58953,"y":0.13504,"w":0.18093,"h":0.02515,"fs":0.01279,"n":3,"k":0,"t":"4p and x2 = -4py."},{"x":0.36275,"y":0.17343,"w":0.59297,"h":0.02815,"fs":0.01279,"n":2,"k":1,"t":"By interchanging the variables x and y, we obtain similar equations for parabolas opening to the right or to the left (Figure 11.39)."},{"x":0.8084,"y":0.23272,"w":0.04725,"h":0.0225,"fs":0.01023,"n":2,"k":1,"t":"Directrix x = p"},{"x":0.43334,"y":0.23369,"w":0.04725,"h":0.01023,"fs":0.01023,"n":1,"k":1,"t":"Directrix"},{"x":0.71243,"y":0.23947,"w":0.05862,"h":0.01461,"fs":0.01023,"n":1,"k":1,"t":"y2 = −4px"},{"x":0.5347,"y":0.24044,"w":0.04772,"h":0.01461,"fs":0.01023,"n":1,"k":1,"t":"y2 = 4px"},{"x":0.43988,"y":0.24543,"w":0.0407,"h":0.01076,"fs":0.01023,"n":1,"k":1,"t":"x = −p"},{"x":0.44519,"y":0.29315,"w":0.03396,"h":0.01023,"fs":0.01023,"n":1,"k":1,"t":"Vertex"},{"x":0.81209,"y":0.29558,"w":0.03396,"h":0.01023,"fs":0.01023,"n":1,"k":1,"t":"Vertex"},{"x":0.74192,"y":0.30567,"w":0.04963,"h":0.02486,"fs":0.01023,"n":2,"k":1,"t":"0 Focus"},{"x":0.4974,"y":0.30664,"w":0.04914,"h":0.02486,"fs":0.01023,"n":2,"k":1,"t":"0 Focus"},{"x":0.72667,"y":0.32135,"w":0.04725,"h":0.01078,"fs":0.01023,"n":1,"k":0,"t":"F(−p, 0)"},{"x":0.51457,"y":0.32285,"w":0.03798,"h":0.01024,"fs":0.01023,"n":1,"k":1,"t":"F(p, 0)"},{"x":0.76053,"y":0.41098,"w":0.01591,"h":0.01023,"fs":0.01023,"n":1,"k":1,"t":"(b)"},{"x":0.51629,"y":0.41195,"w":0.01453,"h":0.01023,"fs":0.01023,"n":1,"k":1,"t":"(a)"},{"x":0.43415,"y":0.4323,"w":0.44549,"h":0.01615,"fs":0.01151,"n":2,"k":1,"t":"FIGURE 11.39 (a) The parabola y2 = 4px. (b) The parabola y2 = -4px."},{"x":0.36275,"y":0.47051,"w":0.47857,"h":0.01795,"fs":0.01407,"n":1,"k":1,"t":"EXAMPLE 1 Find the focus and directrix of the parabola y2 = 10x."},{"x":0.36275,"y":0.50888,"w":0.45198,"h":0.01795,"fs":0.01279,"n":2,"k":1,"t":"Solution We find the value of p in the standard equation y2 = 4px:"},{"x":0.54342,"y":0.54137,"w":0.1891,"h":0.01999,"fs":0.01279,"n":3,"k":0,"t":"4p = 10, so p = 10"},{"x":0.72026,"y":0.54137,"w":0.05074,"h":0.02719,"fs":0.01279,"n":2,"k":0,"t":"4 = 5 2."},{"x":0.36275,"y":0.5818,"w":0.36227,"h":0.01281,"fs":0.01279,"n":1,"k":1,"t":"Then we find the focus and directrix for this value of p:"},{"x":0.08628,"y":0.59606,"w":0.228,"h":0.01023,"fs":0.01023,"n":5,"k":1,"t":"Vertex Vertex Focus Focus Center"},{"x":0.51243,"y":0.60974,"w":0.15642,"h":0.02323,"fs":0.01279,"n":2,"k":0,"t":"Focus: (p, 0) = a5"},{"x":0.66067,"y":0.60974,"w":0.03508,"h":0.03044,"fs":0.01279,"n":1,"k":1,"t":"2, 0b"},{"x":0.17441,"y":0.62455,"w":0.05324,"h":0.01023,"fs":0.01023,"n":1,"k":1,"t":"Focal axis"},{"x":0.51243,"y":0.65391,"w":0.27791,"h":0.01999,"fs":0.01279,"n":4,"k":0,"t":"Directrix: x = -p or x = - 5"},{"x":0.78217,"y":0.66111,"w":0.01397,"h":0.01999,"fs":0.01279,"n":1,"k":1,"t":"2."},{"x":0.08791,"y":0.67092,"w":0.25314,"h":0.02762,"fs":0.01151,"n":3,"k":1,"t":"FIGURE 11.40 Points on the focal axis of an ellipse."},{"x":0.36275,"y":0.69784,"w":0.0596,"h":0.01375,"fs":0.01375,"n":1,"k":1,"t":"Ellipses"},{"x":0.39244,"y":0.74386,"w":0.53246,"h":0.04417,"fs":0.01279,"n":4,"k":1,"t":"DEFINITIONS An ellipse is the set of points in a plane whose distances from two fixed points in the plane have a constant sum. The two fixed points are the foci of the ellipse."},{"x":0.2509,"y":0.76926,"w":0.03488,"h":0.01024,"fs":0.01023,"n":1,"k":1,"t":"P(x, y)"},{"x":0.39244,"y":0.79056,"w":0.53254,"h":0.04351,"fs":0.01279,"n":3,"k":1,"t":"The line through the foci of an ellipse is the ellipse’s focal axis. The point on the axis halfway between the foci is the center. The points where the focal axis and ellipse cross are the ellipse’s vertices (Figure 11.40)."},{"x":0.10719,"y":0.79613,"w":0.13221,"h":0.02879,"fs":0.01023,"n":5,"k":1,"t":"Focus Focus Center 0 F1(−c, 0)"},{"x":0.2122,"y":0.82257,"w":0.03987,"h":0.01216,"fs":0.01023,"n":1,"k":0,"t":"F2(c, 0)"},{"x":0.36275,"y":0.87602,"w":0.58893,"h":0.02882,"fs":0.01279,"n":2,"k":1,"t":"If the foci are F1(-c, 0) and F2(c, 0) (Figure 11.41), and PF1 + PF2 is denoted by 2a, then the coordinates of a point P on the ellipse satisfy the equation"},{"x":0.08823,"y":0.87757,"w":0.25627,"h":0.05832,"fs":0.01261,"n":5,"k":1,"t":"FIGURE 11.41 The ellipse defined by the equation PF1 + PF2 = 2a is the graph of the equation (x2>a2) + (y2>b2) = 1, where b2 = a2 - c2."},{"x":0.51445,"y":0.91758,"w":0.28715,"h":0.01795,"fs":0.01279,"n":1,"k":0,"t":"2(x + c)2 + y2 + 2(x - c)2 + y2 = 2a."}]}